\end{equation}
where $(v/e)_{\text{baseline},\,t}$ is from the single baseline seed (exp\_001, seed~1, 505K steps).  We restrict to a \textbf{35K+ window} ($t \geq 35\text{K}$) because the baseline $v/e$ is near zero in early training, causing spurious $\nve$ inflation.  We use $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ (peak within this window) as a warning threshold; across 12 experimental configurations, this achieves 11/12 accuracy (AUROC${=}0.926$), with one non-CIVO false positive (\S\ref{sec:f6}).  The threshold is sensitive but not perfectly specific to CIVO; mechanistic attribution requires joint analysis with collapse signatures (\S\ref{sec:pathways}).

We distinguish two regimes above the threshold: \textbf{sustained CIVO}, characterized by monotonically escalating $\nve$ culminating in policy collapse (e.g., exp\_008d: $1.78\times \rightarrow 4.12\times$, eval $167 \rightarrow 82.8$), and \textbf{transient exceedance}, where $\nve$ briefly exceeds $1.5\times$ at isolated checkpoints but self-corrects without catastrophic collapse (e.g., exp\_009b: peak $1.62\times$ at 45K, self-correcting to $0.38\times$).  The pathological CIVO diagnosis requires sustained escalation with accompanying performance degradation, not merely a single checkpoint above the threshold.

\paragraph{RSSM fidelity.}
To probe the mechanism underlying value drift, we evaluate world model prediction quality at different temporal scales from the same checkpoint. Specifically, we compute observation MSE and reward MAE on held-out $\dt{=}1$ versus $\dt{=}2$ transitions, and report the gap ratio between them. A large gap indicates that the world model produces systematically different prediction quality across temporal scales; this difference provides the channel for critic exploitation.

\paragraph{Supplementary diagnostics.}
Two additional metrics target failure modes that $\nve$ may miss.
\emph{Actor loss sign}: DreamerV3's actor loss is normally negative (policy improvement direction). A sign flip to positive values directly signals that the critic is misleading the actor and serves as a more targeted marker of delusional-critic damage than the aggregate $\nve$ ratio.
\emph{Value standard deviation}: exponential growth in value variance across imagination rollouts captures oscillatory instability during ``false recovery'' phases where $\nve$ may temporarily decrease even as the underlying value landscape destabilizes.

\subsection{Selective Gradient Stopping}
\label{sec:sgs_method}

To identify which gradient pathway through $e_{\Delta t}$ drives CIVO, we introduce \textit{selective gradient stopping} (SGS): during the backward pass, $e_{\Delta t}$ is detached before entering selected prediction heads via the stop-gradient operator $\operatorname{sg}(\cdot)$, preventing those loss gradients from updating $e_{\Delta t}$'s parameters while preserving its forward-pass contribution.

We test two variants. \textbf{Reward-only SGS} (exp\_016e) detaches $e_{\Delta t}$ from the reward head only: $\hat{r}_t = f_{\text{reward}}(\operatorname{sg}(e_{\Delta t}), h_t, z_t)$, testing whether the reward pathway drives CIVO. \textbf{Full-pathway SGS} (exp\_018) detaches $e_{\Delta t}$ from all non-KL gradient pathways (observation decoders, reward head, and continuation predictor), retaining only the KL dynamics loss signal. To quantify each pathway's contribution, we perform gradient decomposition using \texttt{torch.autograd.grad}, measuring the $\ell_2$ norm of $\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_i$ for each loss component $\mathcal{L}_i$, where $\theta$ denotes the temporal embedding parameters.

\subsection{Environment and Training Details}
\label{sec:env_details}

All experiments use Walker Walk and Cheetah Run from the DeepMind Control Suite \citep{tassa2018dmcontrol} with proprioceptive observations. The baseline runs for 500K environment steps, sufficient for convergence (peak return 970.4 on Walker Walk). Treatment configurations run for 105K steps, which is sufficient to observe both collapse onset and the diagnostic markers described above. All DreamerV3 hyperparameters follow the defaults from \citet{hafner2023dreamerv3} without task-specific tuning. The temporal embedding uses dimension $d{=}32$. Training uses a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (96\,GB) with JIT compilation disabled (\texttt{-{}-compile False}) for reproducibility; wall-clock time is ${\sim}2.5$h per 105K-step run and ${\sim}12$h for the baseline (${\sim}80$ GPU-hours total). The codebase extends the official DreamerV3 implementation\footnote{\url{https://github.com/danijar/dreamerv3}, accessed March 2026.} with the variable-rate modules described in \S\ref{sec:variable_rate}; no other architectural modifications are made.

